# Speaker notes of the Chapter 4 deck v3_8_0, slides 41-79. Reader-facing full sentences, 60-140 words, no presenter directions, no everyday analogies.
NOTES_B = {
41: "Part 4 covers addresses that commit to a script and the second address alphabet. P2SH, pay to script hash, commits to the hash of a whole script: spending reveals the script and then satisfies it. Bech32 addresses are the SegWit addresses, written in lowercase letters and digits with a stronger checksum. Bech32m is the same encoding with a new checksum constant, and it is used from witness version 1. The table at the bottom places this part within the five parts of the chapter. The seventeen slides that follow explain each format and compute its parts.",
42: "The first concept table of this part defines the script hash and the attacks on a hash. Legacy pay to script hash is an address that commits to the hash of a whole script and starts with 3. The redeem script is the script behind the hash, revealed and run when the coins are spent. A collision attack finds any two different inputs with the same hash, and a preimage attack finds an input for one particular hash. Bech32 addresses are the SegWit addresses with a stronger checksum. The attacks return on slides 46 and 47.",
43: "The second concept table of this part defines the terms of the new address format. The problems with Bech32 addresses are that letters inserted before a final p slip past the old checksum. Bech32m is the same encoding with a new checksum constant, used from witness version 1, while bech32 is the original, still used for witness version 0. The witness version is the first value in the address, 0 for the first SegWit and 1 for Taproot. The witness program is the data after the version, and the human-readable part is the letters before the 1, bc or tb.",
44: "Multisignature lets several keys share control, so that no single key decides alone. The script of a two-of-three wallet has 105 bytes: OP_2, three keys of 34 bytes each, OP_3 and the opcode ae, OP_CHECKMULTISIG, which checks m signatures against n public keys. The grid lists all eight sets of signers: four of them pass, namely A with B, A with C, B with C and all three together, and the other four fail. No single stolen or lost key can therefore spend or block the coins. The one-byte parts are drawn wider than to scale.",
45: "P2SH commits to a whole script behind one address. The code builds three public keys, creates a two-of-three multisignature script from them and computes the P2SH address. The script has 105 bytes, and the address is HASH160 of that script with the version byte 05, so it starts with 3 and has 34 characters. The table lists the script size, the hash 15fc0754e73e and the address start. The address 33hG2q39jRi2 commits to the script, and spending reveals all 105 bytes so that the network can check them against the hash.",
46: "Hash attacks differ in cost. The code writes two small helper functions with lambda and applies them to the hash sizes 160 and 256: a collision costs about 2 to the n over 2 tries, so n over 2 bits of work, and a preimage costs about 2 to the n tries, so n bits. The result list [80, 160, 128, 256] shows that a 160-bit hash gives only 80 bits against collisions, while SHA256 gives 128. The chart plots these bits of work. A collision matters for scripts that several parties build together, and a preimage matters for an address that one party made alone.",
47: "Three attacks on a hash need different amounts of work. In a preimage attack the attacker has only the hash and wants any input with that hash: 2 to the 160 tries, or 1.46e+48. In a second preimage attack the attacker has the hash and one input and wants a different input with the same hash, which costs the same. In a collision attack the attacker has nothing and wants any two inputs with one hash, which costs only 2 to the 80, or 1.21e+24. For an address that one party made alone, the chance of success is about 1 in 2 to the 160, so nobody can substitute another key for the one behind it.",
48: "Segregated witness separates the signatures from the transaction identifier. Before the upgrade, the identifier hashed everything, including the signatures, so a third party who re-encoded a signature changed the identifier of an unconfirmed transaction. After the upgrade the signatures move into a separate witness structure, and the identifier hashes only the other fields, while the witness part is committed to separately. A spender, not a third party, now controls the identifier. For this reason chains of unconfirmed payments become safe to build.",
49: "Segwit inside P2SH is a bridge for old wallets. The redeem script is OP_0 followed by the 20-byte key hash: 22 bytes, 0014bbc1e42a and so on. The address is the HASH160 of that script with version 05, and it starts with 3. A wallet that only knows P2SH can pay to it without any change. To spend, the receiver reveals the script and the witness, and segwit nodes check both. The table compares three ways to pay the same key: P2PKH with 34 characters, segwit inside P2SH with 34 characters, and native segwit P2WPKH with 42 characters.",
50: "A Bech32 address can be split into four parts. The code builds a version 0 SegWit address for the key hash of the book's example and splits it at the last 1. The result list shows the human-readable part bc, the version letter q, 32 program letters and the six checksum letters c6aaee. The table explains each part: the network name, the separator, q for witness version 0, 32 letters that stand for 20 bytes, and the checksum. The whole address has 42 characters: bc1q9d3xa5gg45q2j39m9y32xzvygcgay4rgc6aaee.",
51: "The Bech32 checksum is a BCH code, an error-correcting code of six 5-bit letters, which makes 30 bits. It guarantees that any error in four letters or fewer is always caught, and Bitcoin Core can even point to where the error is. The table reports tests on an address of 42 characters: 1,209 single-letter errors in every case were all caught, and 20,000 random errors of two letters and 20,000 of four letters were all caught. With five or more wrong letters the chance of a slip is 1 in 2 to the 30, or 1 in 1,073,741,824.",
52: "A SegWit address carries a witness version and a witness program. The code builds three addresses from the book's programs, a key hash, a script hash and a Taproot key, and decodes each one. The result list [(0, 20), (0, 32), (1, 32)] gives the version and the program length of each. The table shows that P2WPKH and P2WSH both use version 0 and start with bc1q, with 20 and 32 bytes, while P2TR uses version 1 and starts with bc1p. Version 1 changes the letter after the 1 from q to p.",
53: "All three SegWit output types have the same shape: a witness version, a push and a program. P2WPKH is an output script of 22 bytes, the witness version 0 followed by a push of 20 bytes, that commits to one key, as P2PKH does; its address begins bc1q and has 42 characters. P2WSH has 34 bytes and commits to the SHA256 of any script, as P2SH does; its address begins bc1q and has 62 characters. P2TR also has 34 bytes, uses witness version 1 and commits to a key, or more; its address begins bc1p. Version 0 uses the older bech32 checksum and version 1 the corrected bech32m.",
54: "Bech32 and Bech32m differ in one constant, and a small function can tell them apart. The code writes a lambda that tests whether the checksum of a string equals the constant of a format. For the four strings of the book, the first list is all True for the old constant and the second list is all False for the new constant. The table repeats the verdicts by string length: the strings of 15, 18, 20 and 22 characters are accepted as bech32 and rejected as bech32m. Each string has more q letters before the last p, and the old checksum cannot see them.",
55: "The weakness that Bech32m repairs is the length extension: adding or removing a q just before a final p keeps the bech32 checksum valid. The table shows the book's four strings, of 15, 18, 20 and 22 characters, which differ only in their q letters. All four are valid as bech32 and none is valid as bech32m. A typo that adds a letter would therefore go unnoticed in the old format. The fix is a new checksum constant, not a longer address.",
56: "Bech32m changes exactly one constant. Witness version 0 uses the bech32 checksum constant 1, and witness versions 1 to 16 use the bech32m constant 0x2bc830a3, which is 734539939 in decimal. Nothing else in the encoding changes, and the new constant repairs the length-extension weakness. The table lists three addresses from the book: the version 0 key hash and the version 0 script hash are valid with constant 1, and the version 1 Taproot key is valid with 0x2bc830a3. The layout is the same and only one value differs.",
57: "Capitals make smaller QR codes. A QR code is a two-dimensional barcode that a camera can read, and its alphanumeric mode has 45 characters and no lowercase letters. A 42-character bech32 address needs 336 bits in byte mode with lowercase, and 231 bits in alphanumeric mode with capitals, because capitals use 11 bits per two characters against 16. That is 31 percent fewer bits here. The largest QR code holds 4,296 characters in alphanumeric mode against 2,953 in byte mode. Base58 needs both cases, so it falls back to byte mode.",
58: "Part 5 shows how one private key is written for a wallet to import. WIF, the wallet import format, is Base58Check of a version byte 80 and the key. WIF-compressed adds a final 01 byte that marks the key as compressed. A compressed private key is not a smaller key but a flag that tells a wallet to use compressed public keys. The table at the bottom marks this part within the chapter. The four slides of this part define the formats and compute both of them for the book's key.",
59: "This table defines the formats of a private key. One key can be written in several ways: as raw hexadecimal, as WIF and as WIF-compressed. WIF is the Base58Check of a version byte 80 and the key, and WIF-compressed has a final 01 byte that marks the key as compressed. Compressed private keys are not smaller keys: the name is a flag that tells a wallet to use compressed public keys. The next two slides compute both forms and show the two addresses that follow from the flag.",
60: "A WIF string is a private key written in Base58Check. The code writes the book's private key twice: once uncompressed and once compressed. The result tuple ('5', 51, 'K', 52) shows that the first form starts with 5 and has 51 characters, while the compressed form starts with K or L and has 52. The table shows 33 bytes for the first form and 34 for the second. The decoded bytes are 80, the 32-byte key and, when the key is compressed, a final 01.",
61: "One private key gives two addresses. The code hashes the 65-byte uncompressed public key and then the 33-byte compressed key, and the two addresses differ: the first starts 1424C2 and the second 1J7mdg. The compressed flag in WIF tells a wallet which of the two addresses it must watch. The table lists both addresses with their key sizes. Importing a key without knowing the flag can therefore lead to the wrong address and an apparently empty wallet.",
62: "Part 6 covers advanced keys and addresses. A vanity address starts with chosen letters and is found by trying many keys. A paper wallet is a key and its address printed on paper and kept offline. The commitment, a hash that fixes a value now and lets the owner reveal it later, returns in the story of an address that has no key. The table at the bottom places this part within the chapter. The fifteen slides that follow end with the story of the eater address and with the independent checks that earn trust.",
63: "This table defines the advanced concepts of the part. Advanced keys and addresses go beyond a plain random key: they include chosen address prefixes and printed keys. A vanity address starts with chosen letters, found by trying many keys. A paper wallet is a key and its address printed on paper and kept offline. The next slides compute the cost of a vanity search and test a paper wallet before any coins are placed on it.",
64: "A vanity search tries keys until the address starts as chosen. The code tries the keys 1, 2, 3 and so on until the first letter after the 1 is A: it takes 19 tries and finds the address 1Am. Each further chosen letter costs about 58 times more work, because the alphabet has 58 symbols. The chart shows the expected tries per letter. Six chosen letters need 38,068,692,544 tries on average, which is why a search for a long vanity prefix is expensive.",
65: "A paper wallet must give back its address. The code takes the WIF key and the address that would be printed, decodes the WIF text as a scan would, restores the key from bytes 1 to 33 and derives the address again. The comparison returns True. The table runs the steps: print a WIF of 52 characters and an address of 34, scan the version 0x80 and the flag 0x01, restore the key from the WIF text, and check that the address is the same. Only when this reads yes should coins go to the address.",
66: "Bitcoin Core's addresstype option has four values: legacy, p2sh-segwit, bech32 and bech32m. The table gives the output type, the beginning of the address and its length for each: legacy is P2PKH with 34 characters starting 1J7m, p2sh-segwit is P2SH around P2WPKH with 34 characters starting 3FyC, bech32 is P2WPKH with 42 characters starting bc1q, and bech32m is P2TR, a Taproot address, with 62 characters starting bc1p. The default is bech32, highlighted in the table, and the default shows what the reference software regards as current.",
67: "Bitcoin Core 30.0 and later no longer export a single key: dumpprivkey and importprivkey are removed, while getnewaddress and importdescriptors remain. Exporting a single key means taking one private key out of a wallet as a WIF string. In a deterministic wallet, a leaked key plus some public data about the wallet can reveal every other key in it. A single WIF key can still sit inside a descriptor such as wpkh(Kx...), which asks for a P2WPKH output script; importdescriptors takes the whole line, so the wallet knows exactly what it may watch.",
68: "BIP38 protects a private key with a passphrase: something you have, the printed record, plus something you know, the passphrase. The scheme stretches the passphrase and encrypts the 32-byte key, and the result is a Base58Check string of 58 characters that begins 6P. The status in the BIPs repository is Deployed, yet the comment summary reads Unanimously Discourage. The book does not cover it, and you meet it on old paper wallets rather than in new software. The table lists the two parts that the owner needs.",
69: "A deterministic wallet derives every key from one seed. The slide shows a seed beginning f1cc3bc0 and three keys: each is the hash of the seed and a counter, so key 0 is 50b18e0b with the address 1NkD5qxTkPEBs1WGwDQmr33y8xnKGfFh3a, key 1 is a965dbcd and key 2 is 19580c97. Because the procedure is repeatable, one backup of the seed covers every key of the wallet. Chapter 5 shows how wallet recovery works from a word list back to every key.",
70: "Address reuse costs privacy. When one address receives three payments, an onlooker sees all three together and can link every payer. With a fresh address for each payment, three unrelated addresses appear and nothing on the chain joins the payments. Wallets give a new address on every call to getnewaddress. The eater address of the closing story shows the other side: it received 5,832 deposits, and everyone can see them all because they went to one address.",
71: "A hardware signing device keeps the key inside. The computer or phone prepares the unsigned transaction, the device shows the amount and the address for confirmation, the device signs with the key inside, and the computer broadcasts the signed transaction. The less secure program that prepares and broadcasts never sees a key. The only job of the device is to hold private keys and sign with them, and the key never meets general-purpose software.",
72: "Someone spelled a message in base58 letters, 1BitcoinEaterAddressDontSend, and closed it with the six letters f59kuE that make the checksum fit. The result is a valid address whose key nobody holds, because it was chosen as text and not computed from a key. Thousands of anonymous senders paid to it anyway: on 6 October 2026 Blockstream Explorer showed 13.41747849 bitcoin in 5,832 outputs, none ever spent. A checksum catches typing mistakes, not a missing key, so wallets accept the address without a warning.",
73: "The eater address is valid and has no key. The code splits the address after the leading 1: the message is BitcoinEaterAddressDontSend and the closing letters are f59kuE. The check is_valid_b58check returns True, so a wallet accepts the address without any warning. The table shows that the decoded bytes are 21, one version byte and 20 hash bytes. The 20-byte hash 759d6677 was never computed from a key. A valid checksum is therefore not proof that a key exists.",
74: "An implementation earns trust by agreement with independent programs. The BIP350 list holds 7 valid bech32m strings, 8 valid segwit addresses and 15 invalid segwit addresses, each with its reason, and the toolkit of this chapter accepts all 8 valid addresses that were run for this slide. The same address was also produced by this toolkit, by Bitcoin Core 31.1 run as an offline node and by the reference code of the book, and all three agree: 1J7mdg5rbQ. Two programs written apart rarely make the same mistake, so agreement is the evidence.",
75: "A key proves control without a registry. A verifier sends a challenge, here a login line with the date 2026-11-01 and the number 4821. The key holder answers with a signature (r, s) made with the private key. The verifier checks the signature with the public key, and it is valid; another key gives invalid. Whoever holds a private key can prove control of the matching public key by signing, without a central registry or anyone's permission. The same step runs in every Bitcoin payment: a node accepts a spend because the signature verifies, not because anyone vouches for it.",
76: "A Uniform Resource Identifier is a compact sequence of characters that identifies a resource: a web address such as https://example.org/keys/1 has a scheme, an authority and a path. A decentralized identifier has the scheme did, a DID method and an identifier specific to the method, and it associates a subject with a DID document. A DID is built to be decoupled from registries and certificate authorities, and its controller proves control with a key, as a Bitcoin key pair does. The DID document names a verification method, a public key, and a proof of control, a signature made with the matching private key.",
77: "The summary answers the six questions of slide 3. A private key is a random number between 1 and n minus 1 with 256 bits of entropy. The public key is G multiplied by k with doubling and adding, and nobody can run it backwards. The address is HASH160, a version byte and four checksum bytes written in base58. P2SH hashes a script, and Bech32 and Bech32m add a version, a program and a stronger checksum. A key is written down as WIF: Base58Check of version 80, the key and a final 01 when compressed. A checksum catches typos but not a missing key, as the eater address shows.",
78: "The resources slide lists ten places to read, try, watch and ask, and every link was opened on 25 October 2026. Mastering Bitcoin chapter 4 is the text that this deck follows. Elliptic curve cryptography pictures and the Bitcoin Wiki explain the steps from key to address. The standards are BIP 173 for Bech32 and BIP 350 for Bech32m, and the 1976 paper of Diffie and Hellman is the origin of the key pair. The eater address and base58.h let readers inspect the story and the source themselves, and Bitcoin Stack Exchange answers questions.",
79: "Chapter 4 followed one secret number from a private key to an address that can be read aloud. Every result in this deck was computed by the chapter library, not typed. The next chapter, Wallet Recovery, goes from a word list back to every key. Questions about any of the six answers on the summary slide are welcome, and the code of every slide is on the chapter page so that each result can be reproduced.",
}
